2026-09-07 13:59:44 IST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:823 - nsflow: {"type": "AI", "text": "To provide you with an accurate financial breakdown, I have consulted the **CarFinanceAgent**. \n\nBased on a car price of ₹12,00,000 and your down payment of ₹3,00,000, here are the loan details:\n\n### **Loan Summary**\n*   **Loan Amount (Principal):** ₹9,00,000\n*   **Down Payment:** ₹3,00,000\n*   **Interest Rate:** 9% per annum (Fixed)\n*   **Tenure:** 5 Years (60 Months)\n\n### **Financial Breakdown**\n*   **Monthly EMI:** **₹18,682**\n*   **Total Interest Payable:** **₹2,20,926**\n*   **Total Amount Paid (Principal + Interest):** ₹11,20,926\n*   **Total Cost of Ownership (Car Price + Interest):** ₹14,20,926\n\n---\n\n### **Important Notes & Assumptions**\n1.  **Processing Fees:** This calculation does not include bank processing fees or documentation charges, which typically range from **₹2,000 to ₹10,000** or 0.5%–1% of the loan amount.\n2.  **Insurance & Registration:** The ₹12 Lakh price is assumed to be the **On-Road price**. If ₹12 Lakh is the Ex-showroom price, you will need to factor in an additional ₹1.5 Lakh to ₹2 Lakh for RTO, Insurance, and TCS, which would increase your loan amount or down payment requirement.\n3.  **Credit Score:** A 9% interest rate is an estimate for used car loans (which are usually higher than new car loans). If your credit score is excellent, you might negotiate closer to 8.5%. If the score is lower, rates for used cars can go up to 11%–13%.\n4.  **Prepayment:** Most banks allow prepayment after 6–12 EMIs. Doing this can significantly reduce your \"Total Interest Payable.\"\n\n**Would you like me to compare this with a 3-year or 7-year tenure to see how it affects your monthly budget?**"}
