2026-09-07 14:00:17 IST | INFO     | nsflow:_log:823 - nsflow: {"type": "AI", "text": "Since you are inspecting a **2020 Jeep Compass Diesel Automatic** (especially the one priced at ₹13 Lakh, which we identified as suspiciously low), you must be extremely thorough. \n\nI have consulted the **CarInspectionAgent** to create this specific checklist for a 2020 Jeep Compass.\n\n### **1. Documentation & History (Do this first)**\n*   **Service History:** Ask for the digital service logs. Jeep maintenance is strictly interval-based. Look for any mention of \"Transmission Software Update\" or \"DPF Cleaning.\"\n*   **Insurance Claims:** Check the insurance policy. If there is a \"Nil Depreciation\" policy with a claim history, ask for the work orders to see what was replaced.\n*   **Ownership:** Ensure it is not a \"Company Registered\" car that was used as a rough-use site vehicle.\n\n### **2. The Transmission (Critical for 9-Speed AT)**\nThe 9-speed ZF gearbox is the most expensive component to fix.\n*   **The \"P to D\" Test:** With the engine running and your foot on the brake, shift from **P → R → N → D**. The shifts should be silent. If you feel a \"thud\" or a violent jerk, the transmission mounts or the gearbox solenoids may be failing.\n*   **Low-Speed Crawl:** In traffic, check if the car \"hunts\" for gears or jerks between 1st, 2nd, and 3rd gear.\n*   **Highway Shift:** Ensure the car reaches the higher gears (7th, 8th, or 9th) at cruising speeds.\n\n### **3. Engine & BS6 Components**\n*   **DPF Warning:** Look at the instrument cluster for any \"DPF Full\" or \"Engine Check\" lights. \n*   **Cold Start:** Start the car after it has been sitting for a while. Listen for a \"clattering\" sound. While the 2.0L Multijet is noisy, it should not sound metallic or inconsistent.\n*   **Battery Health:** Jeep electronics are very sensitive. Check the battery manufacturing date. If it’s the original 2020 battery, it is due for replacement (Cost: ~₹12,000–₹15,000).\n\n### **4. Suspension & Underbody**\n*   **The \"Thud\" Test:** Drive over a broken patch of road at 20-30 kmph. If you hear a \"clunk\" or \"thud\" from the front, the lower arms or link rods need replacement (a common Compass issue at 50k km).\n*   **Underbody Scrapes:** Jeep owners often take these cars off-road. Inspect the underbody for deep gashes, especially on the fuel tank and oil sump.\n\n### **5. Interior & Features**\n*   **Panoramic Sunroof:** If it has one, open and close it fully. Listen for grinding sounds (indicates sand in the rails) and check the roof liner for water stains (indicates leaking seals).\n*   **Touchscreen (UConnect):** Check if the screen flickers or if the reverse camera lag is excessive.\n*   **AC Performance:** Check if the dual-zone climate control cools both sides equally. A common fault is the \"blend door actuator\" failing, leading to one side being hot and the other cold.\n\n### **6. Specific Red Flags for this Listing**\n*   **Tire Wear:** At 55,000 km, the car should ideally be on its **second set** of tires. If it is still on the original tires, they will be bald and need immediate replacement (Cost: ~₹50,000 for a set of four). If the tires are brand new, check for uneven wear, which indicates alignment or suspension damage.\n*   **Flood Damage:** Check under the seats for rusted bolts and smell the carpets for a damp/musty odor.\n\n---\n\n### **Immediate Verdict Guide for Tomorrow:**\n*   **BUY:** If service history is perfect, no accidents, and the gearbox is smooth. (At 13L, this is a steal).\n*   **NEGOTIATE:** If tires need replacement, minor suspension noise exists, or the battery is weak.\n*   **AVOID:** If there is any jerkiness in the gearbox, \"Check Engine\" light is on, or if the seller cannot provide the service history.\n\n**Would you like me to provide a list of estimated repair costs for common Jeep parts so you can negotiate better?**"}
